{
  "units": [
    {
      "quote": "(1) An applicant with a pending name change must not be issued a passport in her/his new name. See 8 FAM 1001.5 regarding applying for a replacement passport in a new name;",
      "scope_quotes": [
        "c. The court order or decree must be final:\n\n"
      ],
      "kind": "prohibition",
      "meaning": "When an applicant has a pending name change rather than a final court order or decree, passport personnel must not issue a passport in the applicant's new name."
    },
    {
      "quote": "(2) An applicant with a pending name change must not be issued a form FS-240 in her/his new name. See 8 FAM 1001.3 regarding amending a form FS-240; and",
      "scope_quotes": [
        "c. The court order or decree must be final:\n\n"
      ],
      "kind": "prohibition",
      "meaning": "When an applicant has a pending name change rather than a final court order or decree, personnel must not issue a Form FS-240 in the applicant's new name."
    },
    {
      "quote": "(3) You must clear the applicant's pending name even though the name change is not final and even if the passport is not being issued at this time.",
      "scope_quotes": [
        "c. The court order or decree must be final:\n\n"
      ],
      "kind": "requirement",
      "meaning": "Passport personnel must clear the applicant's pending name regardless of the fact that the name change is not final and even if a passport is not being issued at that time."
    },
    {
      "quote": "d. The court order or decree must list both the applicant's current and former name(s).",
      "scope_quotes": [],
      "kind": "requirement",
      "meaning": "A submitted court order or decree is required to list both the applicant's current name and their former name or names."
    },
    {
      "quote": "EXCEPTION: You may receive a court order in which the applicant's former name is made confidential (e.g., California's Safe at Home program).",
      "scope_quotes": [
        "d. The court order or decree must list both the applicant's current and former name(s)."
      ],
      "kind": "exception",
      "meaning": "As an exception to the requirement that a court order or decree list both the current and former name(s), personnel may receive a court order where the applicant's former name has been made confidential."
    },
    {
      "quote": "When this occurs, the applicant must provide both her/his current and former names on the application.",
      "scope_quotes": [
        "d. The court order or decree must list both the applicant's current and former name(s).",
        "EXCEPTION: You may receive a court order in which the applicant's former name is made confidential (e.g., California's Safe at Home program)."
      ],
      "kind": "requirement",
      "meaning": "When a court order renders the applicant's former name confidential, the applicant is obligated to provide both their current and former names on the passport application."
    },
    {
      "quote": "You may request additional evidence of U.S. citizenship/non-citizen U.S. nationality or identity in accordance with 22 CFR 51.23 and 22 CFR 51.45.",
      "scope_quotes": [
        "d. The court order or decree must list both the applicant's current and former name(s).",
        "EXCEPTION: You may receive a court order in which the applicant's former name is made confidential (e.g., California's Safe at Home program)."
      ],
      "kind": "permission",
      "meaning": "When processing a court order with a confidential former name, personnel are authorized to request additional evidence of U.S. citizenship, non-citizen U.S. nationality, or identity under 22 CFR 51.23 and 22 CFR 51.45."
    },
    {
      "quote": "8 FAM 403.1-3(B) Name Spacing\n\n(CT:CITZ-96; 08-04-2023)",
      "scope_quotes": [],
      "kind": "background",
      "meaning": "Provides the section title and administrative transmittal metadata with no operational rights or duties."
    },
    {
      "quote": "a. In accordance with International Civil Aviation Organization (ICAO) recommendations, U.S. passports, form FS-240, and form DS-2060 are printed in all uppercase letters.",
      "scope_quotes": [],
      "kind": "statement",
      "meaning": "Pursuant to ICAO recommendations, all U.S. passports, Form FS-240, and Form DS-2060 are produced with text in all uppercase letters."
    },
    {
      "quote": "Printing a name with multiple parts in uppercase letters causes the name to appear as one word unless the parts are separated with spaces.",
      "scope_quotes": [],
      "kind": "statement",
      "meaning": "Explains the formatting reality that uppercase printing of multi-part names merges parts into a single word unless spaces are inserted between the parts."
    },
    {
      "quote": "Consequently, if the applicant has a name with multiple parts, you must determine how the name will appear:",
      "scope_quotes": [],
      "kind": "requirement",
      "meaning": "Personnel are required to make a determination on how a multi-part name will appear on the issued document."
    },
    {
      "quote": "(1) If the applicant's previous passport had a space, you should add a space on the current application;",
      "scope_quotes": [
        "Consequently, if the applicant has a name with multiple parts, you must determine how the name will appear:"
      ],
      "kind": "recommendation",
      "meaning": "When determining the appearance of a multi-part name, personnel should add a space on the current application if the applicant's previous passport contained a space."
    },
    {
      "quote": "(2) If the applicant does not have a previous passport and the applicant's evidence of U.S. citizenship/non-citizen U.S. nationality or ID has a space, you should add a space on the current application; or",
      "scope_quotes": [
        "Consequently, if the applicant has a name with multiple parts, you must determine how the name will appear:"
      ],
      "kind": "recommendation",
      "meaning": "When determining the appearance of a multi-part name for an applicant lacking a previous passport, personnel should add a space on the current application if their citizenship/nationality evidence or ID has a space."
    },
    {
      "quote": "(3) If the applicant expresses a clear preference to add or remove space, either verbally or in writing, you may add or remove it per the applicant's preference.",
      "scope_quotes": [
        "Consequently, if the applicant has a name with multiple parts, you must determine how the name will appear:"
      ],
      "kind": "permission",
      "meaning": "When an applicant explicitly communicates a preference verbally or in writing to add or remove spacing in a multi-part name, personnel are permitted to add or remove spacing according to that preference."
    },
    {
      "quote": "NOTE: If you receive applications from several family members, you should space the last names consistently, unless a family member expresses a clear preference for a different spacing.",
      "scope_quotes": [],
      "kind": "recommendation",
      "meaning": "Personnel should maintain consistent spacing across last names when receiving applications from multiple family members, unless an individual family member explicitly indicates a clear preference for alternative spacing."
    },
    {
      "quote": "NOTE: For special-issuance passports, the spacing of an eligible family member's last name should match the spacing of the last name of the sponsor.",
      "scope_quotes": [],
      "kind": "recommendation",
      "meaning": "On special-issuance passports, the last name spacing for an eligible family member should conform to the spacing used in the sponsor's last name."
    },
    {
      "quote": "b. Name spacing issues are generally not sufficient cause to rewrite a passport, unless the Department disregarded the applicant's clear preference (see 8 FAM 1001.2).",
      "scope_quotes": [],
      "kind": "exception",
      "meaning": "Issues regarding name spacing generally do not justify rewriting a passport, except when the Department failed to honor the applicant's clearly expressed preference."
    },
    {
      "quote": "c. Arabic ordinal numbers must be changed to Roman numerals, for example, 2nd to II, 3rd to III:",
      "scope_quotes": [],
      "kind": "requirement",
      "meaning": "Personnel are required to convert Arabic ordinal numbers in names into Roman numerals (such as converting 2nd to II, and 3rd to III)."
    },
    {
      "quote": "d. Some applicants may include the prefix “Sr.” as an abbreviation for “Señor” in Spanish meaning “Mr.” or “Mister” in English.",
      "scope_quotes": [],
      "kind": "statement",
      "meaning": "Notes that applicants may use the prefix 'Sr.' to represent the Spanish title 'Señor' rather than an English suffix."
    },
    {
      "quote": "If you determine that this is the case, follow the guidance in 8 FAM 403.1-5(C) regarding ranks and titles.",
      "scope_quotes": [
        "d. Some applicants may include the prefix “Sr.” as an abbreviation for “Señor” in Spanish meaning “Mr.” or “Mister” in English."
      ],
      "kind": "requirement",
      "meaning": "If personnel determine that 'Sr.' is used as an abbreviation for 'Señor', they must follow the instructions governing ranks and titles in 8 FAM 403.1-5(C)."
    },
    {
      "quote": "e. Name suffix issues are generally not sufficient cause to rewrite a passport, unless the Department disregarded the applicant's clear preference (see 8 FAM 1001.2).",
      "scope_quotes": [],
      "kind": "exception",
      "meaning": "Issues concerning name suffixes generally do not constitute sufficient reason to rewrite a passport, unless the Department failed to follow the applicant's clearly expressed preference."
    }
  ]
}
